---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Showcase from "../../../components/Showcase.astro";

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/react`;

const html = `<p>
  A paragraph with a
  <a class="sk-link sk-interactive" href="/en/components/link">
    text color link
  </a>
  and another
  <a
    class="sk-link sk-interactive"
    href="/en/components/link"
    data-tone="primary"
  >
    primary color
  </a>,
  both with permanent underlining.
</p>`;

const react = `import { Link } from "@skryensya/react/link";

<Link href="/en/components/link">Link text color</Link>
<Link href="/en/components/link" tone="primary">primary color</Link>;`;

const tileLinkHtml = `<a
  class="sk-tile sk-tile--interactive sk-interactive"
  href="/usage"
>
  <span class="sk-tile__content">
    <span class="sk-tile__title">Usege details</span>
    <span class="sk-tile__description">
      Open the account usage report.
    </span>
  </span>
</a>`;

const tileLinkVanilla = `import { createTileLink } from "@skryensya/vanilla/tile-link";

const link = createTileLink({ href: "/usage" });
link.textContent = "Usege details";`;

const tileLinkReact = `import { TileLink } from "@skryensya/react/tile-link";

<TileLink href="/usage">
  <span className="sk-tile__content">
    <span className="sk-tile__title">Usege details</span>
    <span className="sk-tile__description">
      Open the account usage report.
    </span>
  </span>
</TileLink>;`;
---

<Base title="Link" description="Link: a text link, always underlined, the only treatment that WCAG 1.4.1 allows.">
  <h1>Link</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Link preserves the native semantics of <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>: use it for navigation and provide a
    <code>href</code> valid. It is a <strong>text link</strong> with permanent underline, the only one
    treatment that WCAG 1.4.1 allows in prose. The hover uses the state layer (<code>sk-interactive</code>),
    not a color invented by the component.
  </p>

  <Showcase html={html} react={react} label="Link" />

  <h2>Surface Link: TileLink</h2>
  <p>
    When an entire surface is a single destination, use <code>TileLink</code>. It also renders a
    <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>, but it is not <code>sk-link</code>: it preserves the Tile geometry and its state layer
    because context, not prose underlining, communicates that the surface is navigable.
  </p>
  <Showcase html={tileLinkHtml} react={tileLinkReact} label="TileLink" />
  <p>Authored HTML works without initialization. <code>createTileLink</code> only creates the anchor when the tree is generated from JavaScript.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={tileLinkVanilla} label="JavaScript" />

  <h2>Why just one type, and not three?</h2>
  <p>
    A link within a text block <strong>cannot be distinguished by color alone</strong>: is
    <a class="sk-link sk-interactive" href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/use-of-color.html">WCAG 1.4.1
    (Use of Color)</a>, level A. There are only two ways to comply: a non-color indicator
    <em>at rest</em>, a permanent underline, or a contrast of ≥3:1 between the link color and the
    of the text <em>plus</em> a non-color signal on hover <em>and</em> focus.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second way is fragile: you have to re-measure that 3:1 in each brand and each mode, and whoever reads the page
    still, or cannot hover, it does not receive any signal until touching the link. An underlining that
    appears only on hover, leaves the text at rest without a clue, and without underlining the criterion of
    flat. So <code>sk-link</code> has <strong>a</strong> form, always underlined, and not a hook
    to turn it off: offering <code>hover</code> or <code>none</code> would be offering a way to fail 1.4.1.
  </p>
  <div class="sk-alert" data-tone="info" data-presentation="accent">
    <div class="sk-alert__content">
    <p>
      <strong>And the links that are not underlined?</strong> A navigation item, a breadcrumb, the
      prev/next of the footer, those are distinguished by <em>location</em>, not by color, so 1.4.1 does not
      asks for underlining. But they are not <code>sk-link</code>: they use the <code>nav-list</code> pattern or a button
      <code>ghost</code>. <code>sk-link</code> is, by definition, the <em> link within the </em> text.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The tone does not change the rule</h2>
  <p>
    By default the link takes the same <strong> color as the text</strong> and relies entirely on the
    underlined. <code>data-tone="primary"</code> paints it the primary brand color. In both cases the
    underlining is mandatory: without it, the primary tone would only be distinguished by color (WCAG 1.4.1 bug),
    and the default would not be distinguished from the prose.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of the preview.</p>

  <h2>Contrato</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Renders a native anchor: <code>href</code> defines the target.</li>
    <li><code>data-tone</code> is optional: without it, the text color; <code>primary</code> uses the primary brand color.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>The underline is not configurable</strong>: it is always there, because a text link without a signal
      permanent non-color fails WCAG 1.4.1.
    </li>
    <li>It does not set <code>target</code>, <code>rel</code> or any behavior for external links.</li>
    <li>
      It takes <code>sk-interactive</code>: hover, press and focus come from the state layer. The
      <code>::before</code> opens a little wider than the ink so the wash does not read like a
      stain on the glyphs; the permanent underline remains the idle signal.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>
